meta {
  name: Sign in - wrong password
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/sessions
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "email": "{{failedSignInEmail}}",
    "password": "not the right password"
  }
}

script:pre-request {
  // Prefer the address `Register` just made, and fall back to the seeded one
  // when this request is run on its own. The reason is the per-address
  // limiter: five failures an hour against one address is five, and pointing
  // every run at `dev@snip.test` would make the collection stop being
  // re-runnable after the fifth pass. A fresh address per run has a fresh
  // counter.
  bru.setVar("failedSignInEmail", bru.getVar("newAccountEmail") || bru.getEnvVar("email"));
}

tests {
  test("refuses without saying which half was wrong", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(401);
    expect(res.body.error.code).to.equal("invalid_credentials");
    expect(res.body.error.message).to.equal("Invalid email or password");
  });
}

docs {
  FR-036. `Account.authenticate_by` runs a bcrypt comparison against a dummy
  digest when no account matches, so an unknown address and a wrong password
  cost the same time — and the body here is byte-identical to the one an
  unknown address produces. Change the email to something that has never
  existed and watch nothing about the response change.

  The same body is returned with a 429 once the limiter fires: an attacker who
  could tell "throttled" from "wrong password" would have learned that the
  limiter counting this address had been touched. Only the status differs.

  Try it: send this five times and watch the status become 429 while the JSON
  stays the same. That exhausts the hour for whichever address it ran against,
  which is why it does not run against the seeded one when it can avoid it.
}
